WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.360
INTRO:
Amazon has become the go to place for convenient&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:09.060
online shopping around the world. It usually&nbsp;
only takes a few clicks to place an order and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:15.600
the item will be delivered to your doorstep within&nbsp;
48 hours if not 24 hours. Amazon’s relentless push&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.640
to perfect their logistics and bring down delivery&nbsp;
times has put them a league ahead of all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:26.280
competition. Not to mention their customer service&nbsp;
which is generally top of the line. Considering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:31.920
Amazon’s convenience, speed, and variety it’s not&nbsp;
surprising that Amazon is able to pull in nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:38.700
$500 billion worth of revenue on an annual basis.&nbsp;
This makes them the third largest company in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:44.760
world by revenue only beaten out by Walmart and&nbsp;
Saudi Aramco. And honestly, it’s just a matter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:51.600
of time until they become #1. With all that being&nbsp;
said though, Amazon has a serious quality control&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:58.200
issue. Now, I’m not trying to hate on Amazon.&nbsp;
I’m actually a massive fan of Amazon stock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:02.580
and if you’re strictly buying branded products&nbsp;
from Logitech or Corsair through Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:08.340
the experience is perfect. If you’re buying&nbsp;
unbranded or private label products, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:14.400
it’s a completely different story. More times&nbsp;
than not, these products are simply low quality&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:19.440
Chinese products sourced through Alibaba with&nbsp;
insane markups to make up for Amazon’s fees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:25.740
shipping, marketing, and profit. One of the worst&nbsp;
offenders on Amazon is cheap electronics and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:32.640
notably dongles. Personally, I’ve experienced this&nbsp;
first hand with USB C dongles. I’ve gone through 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:38.520
of these dongles within the past 21 months. They&nbsp;
simply stop working after couple of months, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:45.000
judging by the reviews, this is not an uncommon&nbsp;
problem. This is honestly super frustrating when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:49.980
you’re shelling out $20-$30 every 6 months just&nbsp;
so you can plug in your keyboard and mouse to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.540
your computer. But, when you take a look at how&nbsp;
much it costs to produce these pieces of trash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:02:01.860
it’s not suprirsing that they’re so garbage. This&nbsp;
USB C hub on Amazon, for example, costs $25. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:08.460
same product on Ali Express only costs $10. And&nbsp;
an even better hub on Alibaba only costs 10 to 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:14.760
cents. And I’m not cherry picking a dongle either.&nbsp;
There are dozens of dongles on Alibaba that cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:21.660
just 10 to 20 cents. Now granted, you do have&nbsp;
to make a minimum order of 200 units on Alibaba,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:27.000
but at 20 cents a piece, that’s only $40&nbsp;
total. Even with international shipping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:32.520
you can easily get your hands on 200 of these for&nbsp;
less than a 100 bucks which is a net unit cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:36.900
of less than 50 cents per unit. If you were&nbsp;
an Amazon seller buying thousands of these,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:42.300
you could probably source them for 25 cents&nbsp;
per unit. So, when customers are paying $25&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:47.280
for a product that only costs 25 cents, it’s&nbsp;
no wonder why the product doesn’t stack up to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:51.840
their quality expectations. Efficient market&nbsp;
hypothesis would tell you that excess profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:56.580
margins usually disappear with time as more&nbsp;
competitors enter the market. These dongles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:01.860
however, have plenty of competition&nbsp;
yet all of them cost $20-$30. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:06.480
where in the world is all this money going if&nbsp;
not into the quality of the product itself.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.100
THE FBA ABYSS:&nbsp;

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:16.020
One of the biggest issues plaguing Amazon’s&nbsp;
private label business is simply how the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:21.120
service is structured. Usually, most private&nbsp;
label businesses use something called Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:27.240
FBA. This is when you source products from Alibaba&nbsp;
and then ship the inventory directly to Amazon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:31.980
Amazon will take care of sorting the products,&nbsp;
distributing them across America, and shipping&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.980 --> 00:03:37.380
them to customers within their insane timeframes.&nbsp;
Honestly, the fact that Amazon is able to handle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:42.660
so many products and ship them to so many&nbsp;
people so quickly is truly a logistics marvel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:49.560
but it’s also extremely expensive. Not only does&nbsp;
Amazon charge us the customers for Amazon Prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:56.400
or shipping fees, but they also charge sellers an&nbsp;
Amazon FBA fee. Right now, the absolute smallest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:03.060
fee that Amazon charges sellers is $3.07 per unit.&nbsp;
And that’s for extremely small and light objects.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:08.100
To be categorized as a small standard-size, the&nbsp;
shortest side of the given product has to be less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:13.680
than 0.75 inches. Fortunately, the USB C cable&nbsp;
that we were looking at would fall under this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:19.560
definition. But, there’s plenty of small items&nbsp;
that don’t. Take this tiny pot, for example which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.560 --> 00:04:24.960
only measures 2 inches on every side. Most&nbsp;
of us would classify this as a small item,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.960 --> 00:04:30.900
but Amazon would actually classify it as a large&nbsp;
standard item. And if the product has some heft to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.900 --> 00:04:37.800
it as in 2-3 pounds, sellers can easily end up&nbsp;
paying $6.08 per unit. And that’s simply for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:43.020
small item. If you’re actually selling a medium&nbsp;
sized item, it’ll be classified as an oversize&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.020 --> 00:04:48.600
item for which Amazon will charge a minimum of $10&nbsp;
per unit. Sellers pay these fees for every unit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:54.420
they sell from the backend, and they of course&nbsp;
just pass on these fees to us the customers. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.420 --> 00:05:01.140
really, we’re paying for logistics twice. For your&nbsp;
average $20 product, $3 to $6 goes to paying the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.140 --> 00:05:06.300
Amazon FBA fee. And given that most products&nbsp;
do not fit within the small standard size,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:11.820
the average fee is skewed towards $6. For&nbsp;
simplicity, we’ll call it $5 on average.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:18.300
Spoiler alert, that’s just the first fee. Amazon&nbsp;
also charges sellers a referral fee. The reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:23.100
that Amazon FBA businesses are able to get off&nbsp;
the ground so quickly is because Amazon already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.100 --> 00:05:28.680
has a massive customer base. And to gain access to&nbsp;
this customer base, you have to pay Amazon 8-15%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.280 --> 00:05:36.180
of your total sale price. So, for a $20 product,&nbsp;
this works out to anywhere between $1.60 and $3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.180 --> 00:05:41.940
We’ll estimate in the middle and call it $2.30.&nbsp;
Tack this onto the FBA fee and now we’re looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:48.976
at a total of $7.30 per unit. Moving on, we have&nbsp;
inventory storage fees. This fee is pretty self&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.976 --> 00:05:53.640
explanatory. Amazon charges sellers to be able&nbsp;
to store their products at Amazon warehouses.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.640 --> 00:06:00.600
Between January and September, this fee isn’t&nbsp;
too bad coming at 83 center per cubic foot. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:06.420
during the holiday months, this fee triples&nbsp;
to $2.40 per cubic foot. On top of all this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.420 --> 00:06:10.920
Amazon also charges a $40 per month fee for&nbsp;
simply being a professional seller on Amazon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.640 --> 00:06:16.920
That wraps all of the required fees that all&nbsp;
Amazon FBA sellers have to deal with, but some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:21.900
sellers may be subject to additional fees as well.&nbsp;
For example, when you ship products to Amazon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:27.660
they want each product labeled and barcoded in a&nbsp;
specific way. You can either pay your supplier to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:32.880
do this which is definitely the cheaper option or&nbsp;
you can pay Amazon to do this. If you want Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:39.300
to do this or if your supplier did it wrong,&nbsp;
Amazon will charge you a minimum of $1.25 per&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:44.820
unit. Additionally, sellers are also subject to&nbsp;
a product return fee, an inventory removal fee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.820 --> 00:06:50.040
and other penalties. When you add all of this&nbsp;
together, you realize that for an average $20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:55.800
product, the seller is paying Amazon $10 off&nbsp;
the top line for logistics and seller fees. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.800 --> 00:07:01.320
other words, 50% of the products purchase price&nbsp;
is not even going towards the product itself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:06.600
It’s going towards making the product available&nbsp;
on Amazon and then getting it to you. Now again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:11.040
I’m not trying to bag on Amazon.&nbsp;
Logistics, especially the way Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:16.920
does it is expensive. But, as a customer just&nbsp;
know that this is what you’re really paying for.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:21.240
THE MARKETING ABYSS:
A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:28.320
$10 fee for a $20 product is no doubt quite&nbsp;
expensive, but that still leaves $10. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:32.160
seller should be able to spend $5 on the product&nbsp;
itself and then profit $5 for themselves right?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.160 --> 00:07:37.500
That would be a win win scenario. Amazon gets&nbsp;
their fees, the seller makes their profits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:42.780
and the customer gets atleast a decent product,&nbsp;
but the thing that you’re forgetting about is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:48.180
marketing. Now, some bigger private label brands&nbsp;
go out and run Google ads, Facebook ads, and even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:54.300
YouTube ads. But, by far the most effective type&nbsp;
of marketing Amazon ads. Whenever you search for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:59.580
something on Amazon, you know how the top search&nbsp;
results say “Sponsored”. Well, these are Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:05.340
ads and they're extremely expensive because&nbsp;
they’re extremely effective. If you were to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.340 --> 00:08:10.860
see an ad before this video, maybe 1 in a 1000&nbsp;
of you would actually go buy the product. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:14.760
whenever you put a product at the top of Amazon&nbsp;
search in front of people who are already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:19.920
searching for the product and ready to buy, the&nbsp;
conversion rate goes through the roof. The average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.920 --> 00:08:25.440
conversion rate across Amazon is 10-15% and the&nbsp;
average conversion rate across Prime members&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:32.040
is a mind boggling 74%. To capture these insane&nbsp;
conversions, however, you have to be at the top of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:39.540
Amazon search. 60% of all Amazon sales go to the&nbsp;
first 3 listings and 80% go to the first page. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:44.340
if you’re launching a new Amazon product,&nbsp;
you basically have to pay for Amazon ads to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.340 --> 00:08:50.520
get any sort of traction. The average cost per&nbsp;
click across Amazon is $0.89. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:55.440
everytime someone clicks on a sponsored&nbsp;
listing, the seller has to pay Amazon 89 cents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.440 --> 00:09:01.320
and that’s just the average. I used to sell on&nbsp;
Amazon myself in moderately competitive niches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:07.440
and to secure the first spot in those niches,&nbsp;
it cost $3 per click and that was back in 2019.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.600
With the influx of online shopping due to the&nbsp;
pandemic, I wouldn’t be surprised if that number&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:18.840
was closer to $5 per click today especially for&nbsp;
something as competitive as USB C dongles. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:23.880
even if we estimate conservatively and call it&nbsp;
89 cents per click, if you have a conversion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:29.760
rate of 10%, it’s gonna cost you $8.90 to&nbsp;
move a single unit. Combine this with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.880
FBA fees and you’ll be paying Amazon $18.90 per&nbsp;
unit and that’s not even including the cost of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:40.740
the product. Realistically though, there’s no&nbsp;
way, you’ll only be paying 89 cents per click&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.740 --> 00:09:45.480
especially when you launch. If you want your&nbsp;
listing to appear within the top few spots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.480 --> 00:09:52.080
you’re likely gonna have to pay $3 per click. Even&nbsp;
if you have a 20% conversion rate, that works out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:58.740
to $15 per unit. Combine this with the FBA fee and&nbsp;
you’re up to $25 per unit, you’re $5 in the red,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:04.560
and you haven’t even paid for the product yet. And&nbsp;
this is why private label products on Amazon are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:09.300
so garbage. Given how much sellers have to pay for&nbsp;
FBA and how much they have to pay for marketing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.300 --> 00:10:14.760
they have no money left to actually pay for the&nbsp;
product itself. You can’t feel too bad for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.760 --> 00:10:19.140
sellers though because if they do make it to&nbsp;
the otherside and rank their product highly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.140 --> 00:10:24.780
they end up making bank. At this point, they&nbsp;
can substantially scale back on the Amazon ads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.780 --> 00:10:30.360
to maybe the average of 89 cents per click. If&nbsp;
they maintain the 20% conversion rate, this comes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:38.460
out to $4.45 per click. Combine this with the FBA&nbsp;
fee and they’re up to $14.45 per unit. If we say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:44.400
the product costs 2.50 cents per unit, they’re&nbsp;
all in cost is roughly $17 per unit. At a sale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:50.340
price of $20, they’ll be clearing $3 on every&nbsp;
unit. And the top sellers in the popular niches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.340 --> 00:10:55.680
generally sell well over 10,000 units per month&nbsp;
which works out to $30,000 per month in profit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.680 --> 00:11:01.560
off of just one product. If you replicate this&nbsp;
success with 10 products, you’ll be making $3.6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:08.100
million per year. I think you can see why Amazon&nbsp;
FBA is such a popular business. But, in terms of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:12.840
the quality of the product, by the time you finish&nbsp;
paying Amazon and yourself, there’s nothing left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:19.280
to spend on the product itself. And this is why&nbsp;
private label products on Amazon are so garbage.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:25.680
THE AMAZON:
From all of this, the number one thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:31.380
that we can takeaway is that the vast majority of&nbsp;
the purchase price goes directly to Amazon. For a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:36.900
$20 product, it’s extremely common for 75% of the&nbsp;
proceeds to go to Amazon between the selling fees,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:42.780
the FBA fees, and the marketing fees. But, the&nbsp;
funny part is that Amazon isn’t profiting from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.780 --> 00:11:48.840
any of this either. In fact, they’re literally&nbsp;
losing money. Within the first 6 months of 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.840 --> 00:11:55.140
Amazon lost $5 billion on their retail business.&nbsp;
And the main reason for this is that it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.140 --> 00:11:59.880
extraordinarily expensive to pay for hundreds of&nbsp;
thousands of warehouse workers, delivery drivers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:05.220
pilots, planes, trucks, ships, factories,&nbsp;
warehouses, boxes, packaging materials, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:10.680
everything else that goes along with logistics. By&nbsp;
the end of it, neither Amazon nor their employees&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.680 --> 00:12:15.900
are left with that much money and customers&nbsp;
are left with trash products. The only people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:20.640
who come out on top are Amazon shareholders and&nbsp;
the select FBA sellers who make it to the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.640 --> 00:12:26.100
side. The obvious solution to this conundrum is&nbsp;
to simply spend less money on logistics and more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:31.440
money on the products themselves. But, with&nbsp;
our increasingly short attention spans, this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:38.640
is simply unfeasible. So maybe, the problem isn’t&nbsp;
Amazon or FBA sellers or the products themselves.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.640 --> 00:12:44.400
Maybe, the real problem is actually just our&nbsp;
insatiable desire for instant gratification.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.400 --> 00:12:49.200
But that’s just what I think. Have you had a bad&nbsp;
experience with a private label Amazon product?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.200 --> 00:12:54.480
Comment that down below. Also, drop a like if&nbsp;
you hope that Amazon does something about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:58.740
quality issue. And of course, consider checking&nbsp;
out our discord community to suggest future video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:02.040
ideas and consider subscribing to see&nbsp;
more questions logically answered.

